{"id":"ak-12","slug":"ak-12","name":"AK-12","aliases":["AK15","AK12","AK-19","Aku12","Ak12c","Dbv12","Rpk12","AKU-12","PPK-12","RPK-12","SVD-12","AK-308","AK-12K","AK-15K","AK-400","Ak12br","AK-12/76","AK-15 rifle","AK-15 (rifle)","Kalashnikov AK-12","AK-200 Kalashnikov"],"kind":"firearm","category":"assault-rifles","subcategory":"Russian assault rifle and variants","description":"The AK-12 is a Russian gas-operated assault rifle chambered in 5.45×39mm cartridge, designed and manufactured by Kalashnikov Concern, making it the fifth generation of Kalashnikov rifles.","history":"The AK-12 project began in 2011 by the IZHMASH factory, which became part of the Kalashnikov Concern as a private venture, in an attempt to participate in the \"Ratnik\" trials which were held by the Russian army. The original design of the AK-12 went through three different prototype modifications to improve upon the range of defects discovered in the original design, to meet the Russian military's standard and to address the Russian army's concerns regarding the cost and issues in fully automatic fire. When Kalashnikov Concern took over development, the original prototype designs were later abandoned in favour of the well-proven design of the AK-400 prototype, which took over the \"AK-12\" designation and became the basis for the finalised model of the AK-12.","development":"On 25 May 2010, the Russian media published a Russian Ministry of Defence statement that the AK-12 was to be tested in 2011. The first prototype was presented to the Russian Prime Minister Vladimir Putin during his official visit to inspect the products of the Izhmash arms manufacturing plant in Izhevsk in February 2012. The prototype was fitted with a large-capacity 60-round casket magazine. On the early prototype model, the traditional locations of the cocking handle, safety lever, and fire selector remained unchanged, but the AK-12's production model featured revisions to all of these features.\nIn June 2012, the AK-12 was unveiled to the public for the first time at the defence exhibition in Moscow, Engineer Technologies 2012. On 16 September 2013, the Deputy Chairman of the Military-Industrial Commission of Russia said the Russian Army would start receiving AK-12 assault rifles chambered in 5.45 mm and 7.62 mm in 2014. The new rifle would be put into service along with the new handguns, machine guns and sniper rifles. The AK-12 basic platform allows for nearly 20 different modifications to change into other configurations.\nState trials were to begin in fall 2013. However, on 23 September 2013, the Izvestiya tabloid wrote that, according to an anonymous source, the AK-12 will not be adopted or even undergo state tests due to shortcomings in preliminary tests. The AK-12 was intended to replace three previous generations of AK models and to be standardised as the primary assault rifle of the Russian military. The government rejected the AK-12 because senior commanders said they had millions of stockpiled AK-74s and did not need a new rifle. However, trials would continue for law enforcement agencies.\nHowever, on 23 December 2014, the Russian Army announced that the AK-12, as well as the 6P67 KORD, had passed state trials and would be accepted into service with operational units for evaluation. It was expected that both rifles would begin being trialled operationally by Russian forces by March 2015.\nOn 6 September 2016, it was reported that Kalashnikov Concern introduced the final production model of the AK-12, which was derived from the AK-400 prototype model and has replaced the earlier prototype models. There were two base models that were introduced, the AK-12 which is chambered in 5.45×39mm cartridge and the AK-15 which is chambered in 7.62×39mm cartridge. Kalashnikov Concern also introduced a new light machine gun that is chambered in 5.45×39mm cartridge, the RPK-16 which is based on the traditional Kalashnikov layout and design and has several novel technical and ergonomic features derived from the AK-12 program.\nIt was also reported that the final production model of the AK-12 and AK-15 began participating in troop trials with the Russian Army, where it competed against the Degtyarov 6P67 KORD and 6P68 KORD balanced action assault rifles. The AK-12 completed its operational testing and passed military field tests in June 2017. Both AK-12 and AK-15 completed testing in December 2017.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"5.45×39mm (AK-12), 7.62×39mm (AK-15), 5.56×45mm NATO (AK-19), 7.62×51mm NATO (AK-308)"},{"label":"Action","value":"Gas-operated long-stroke piston system, closed rotating bolt"},{"label":"Rate of Fire","value":"700 rounds/min","numeric":700,"unit":"rounds/min"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"880–900 m/s (AK-12), 715 m/s (AK-15), 910 m/s (AK-19)"},{"label":"Sights","value":"Back-up iron sights and integrated Picatinny rail for various optical sights"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"3.7 kg empty (AK-12)","numeric":3.7,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Weight","value":"3.75 kg empty (AK-15)","numeric":3.75,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Weight","value":"3.8 kg empty (AK-19)","numeric":3.8,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Weight","value":"4.3 kg empty (AK-308)","numeric":4.3,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Length","value":"922 mm full length, 688 mm stock folded","numeric":922,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"415 mm","numeric":415,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"290 mm (AK-12K, AK-15K)","numeric":290,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"228 mm (AK-12SK, AK-15SK)","numeric":228,"unit":"mm"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Effective Range","value":"440 m point blank (AK-12), 350 m point blank (AK-15), 500 m (AK-19)","numeric":440,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Maximum Range","value":"3150 m","numeric":3150,"unit":"m"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"Mikhail Kalashnikov, Vladimir Zlobin, Sergey Urzhumcev"},{"label":"Designed","value":"2011"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Izhmash (now Kalashnikov Concern)"},{"label":"Produced","value":"2018–present"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"150,000+"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Russia"},{"label":"In Service","value":"2018–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"5.45×39mm (AK-12), 7.62×39mm (AK-15), 5.56×45mm NATO (AK-19), 7.62×51mm NATO (AK-308)","Action":"Gas-operated long-stroke piston system, closed rotating bolt","Rate of Fire":"700 rounds/min","Muzzle Velocity":"880–900 m/s (AK-12), 715 m/s (AK-15), 910 m/s (AK-19)","Sights":"Back-up iron sights and integrated Picatinny rail for various optical sights","Weight":"3.7 kg empty (AK-12)","Length":"922 mm full length, 688 mm stock folded","Barrel Length":"415 mm","Effective Range":"440 m point blank (AK-12), 350 m point blank (AK-15), 500 m (AK-19)","Maximum Range":"3150 m","Designer":"Mikhail Kalashnikov, Vladimir Zlobin, Sergey Urzhumcev","Designed":"2011","Manufacturer":"Izhmash (now Kalashnikov Concern)","Produced":"2018–present","Number Built":"150,000+","Origin":"Russia","In Service":"2018–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/5.45mm_assault_rifle_6P70_02.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5.45mm_assault_rifle_6P70_02.jpg","width":5184,"height":3456,"caption":"5.45mm assault rifle AK-12 (6P70).","license":"CC0","licenseUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","attribution":"Nickel nitride"}},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/5.45mm_assault_rifle_6P70_02.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5.45mm_assault_rifle_6P70_02.jpg","width":5184,"height":3456,"caption":"5.45mm assault rifle AK-12 (6P70).","license":"CC0","licenseUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","attribution":"Nickel nitride"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons